          ],
          "content": "104"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.138,
            0.559,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Using the distance formula, we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.167,
            0.637,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "AB = \\sqrt{\\left(6-3\\right)^{2}+\\left(4-1\\right)^{2}}=\\sqrt{9+9}=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.202,
            0.635,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{BC} = \\sqrt{\\left( 8 - 6 \\right)^{2} + \\left( 6 - 4 \\right)^{2}} = \\sqrt{4 + 4} = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.237,
            0.664,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": "AC  = \\sqrt{ \\left( 8 - 3 \\right)^{2} + \\left( 6 - 1 \\right)^{2} } = \\sqrt{ 25 + 25 } = \\sqrt{ 50 } = 5\\sqrt{ 2 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.275,
            0.901,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": "Since, $AB + BC = 3\\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{2} = 5\\sqrt{2} = AC$ ,  we can say that the points A, B and C are collinear. Therefore, they are seated in a line."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.333,
            0.901,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4 :** Find a relation between x and y such that the point (x , y) is equidistant from the points (7, 1) and (3, 5)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.378,
            0.789,
            0.397
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Let $\\mathrm { P } ( x , y )$ be equidistant from the points A(7, 1) and B(3, 5)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.402,
            0.515,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "We are given that $\\mathrm{AP} = \\mathrm{BP}. \\mathrm{So}, \\mathrm{AP}^2 = \\mathrm{BP}^2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.429,
            0.139,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.292,
            0.427,
            0.675,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": "(x - 7)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} = (x - 3)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.454,
            0.139,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.453,
            0.758,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": "x^{2}-14x+49+y^{2}-2y+1=x^{2}-6x+9+y^{2}-10y+25",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.479,
            0.14,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.479,
            0.514,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": "x - y = 2",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.512,
            0.385,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": "which is the required relation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.544,
            0.518,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "**Remark :** Note that the graph of the equation $x - y = 2$ is a line. From your earlier studies, you know that a point which is equidistant from A and B lies on the perpendicular bisector of AB. Therefore, the graph of $x - y = 2$ is the perpendicular bisector of AB (see Fig. 7.7)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.687,
            0.516,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5 :** Find a point on the y-axis which is equidistant from the points $\\mathrm { A } ( 6 ,   5 )$ and B(– 4, 3)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.524,
            0.509,
            0.898,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.751,
            0.516,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** We know that a point on the y-axis is of the form (0, y). So, let the point $\\mathrm { P } ( 0 , y )$ be equidistant from A and B. Then"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.662,
            0.784,
            0.748,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 7.7"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.291,
            0.813,
            0.691,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": "( 6 - 0 ) ^ { 2 } + ( 5 - y ) ^ { 2 } = ( - 4 - 0 ) ^ { 2 } + ( 3 - y ) ^ { 2 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_22.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.838,
            0.139,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.288,
            0.837,
            0.654,
            0.857
          ],
          "content": "36+25+y^{2}-10y=16+9+y^{2}-6y",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.862,
            0.139,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.439,
            0.861,
            0.525,
            0.88
          ],
          "content": "4 y = 3 6",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_26.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.886,
            0.14,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
